id: pm-slack-standup-j82b
title: "The Windows command-line quoting is quadratic on a backslash run, and a workspace path is exactly the string that carries one"
description: "CodeQL alert 1 reports js/polynomial-redos at index.ts line 960, inside quoteWindowsArg. The escaping was two chained replace calls, /(\\\\*)\"/g and /(\\\\*)$/, and both are quadratic on a run of backslashes: the engine retries the pattern at every position in the run, and the greedy quantifier rescans the remainder of the run on each attempt.\n\nThe input is reachable rather than theoretical, which is what raises this above a lint note. quoteWindowsArg renders each element of the cmd.exe tail that pm is launched through on Windows, and the tracker root reaches that tail through --pm-path. A Windows path is precisely the kind of string that carries long backslash runs, so the adversarial shape here is not exotic input — it is a deep path.\n\nMeasured on a 100002-character argument shaped as a space, an x and a hundred thousand backslashes: 16202 ms before, 5 ms after. The fix replaces both expressions with one left-to-right pass that applies the identical CommandLineToArgvW rules — a run of n backslashes before a quote becomes 2n plus one and a literal quote, a run at the end of the element doubles because the closing quote is also a quote the parser sees, and backslashes elsewhere pass through untouched. The rules did not change, only how they are applied."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[1]: security
created_at: "2026-09-03T17:56:47.101Z"
updated_at: "2026-09-03T18:45:48.703Z"
closed_at: "2026-09-03T17:57:04.097Z"
completed_at: "2026-09-03T17:57:04.097Z"
author: pi-agent
acceptance_criteria: "The adversarial argument returns within a hard time bound rather than merely faster; the escaping is asserted for correctness in the same test rather than only for speed, so a fast but wrong implementation fails; the regression test fails when the original expressions are restored, verified by reverting; and CodeQL confirms alert 1 closed on a scan of main"
risk: high
resolution: "Replaced two chained quadratic replace calls with one linear left-to-right pass applying identical CommandLineToArgvW escaping rules. The rules did not change, only how they are applied."
expected_result: "A 100002-character backslash-heavy argument renders within a hard time bound, the doubling rule still holds, and CodeQL alert 1 closes on a scan of main."
actual_result: "The witness renders in 5ms where it previously took 16202ms. The regression test asserts the doubling rule alongside the bound, and was proved to fail against the original expressions by restoring them."
comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-09-03T17:57:02.750Z",codex,"Proved the regression test non-vacuous by reverting rather than asserting it. With the original two replace calls restored and the new test in place, the test fails at 16202.57ms against its 50ms bound; with the linear pass restored it passes at 4.99ms. The test also asserts the escaping itself — that a run of n backslashes at the end of the element doubles to 2n before the closing quote — so an implementation that were fast but wrong would fail it too. A speed-only assertion would have accepted a rewrite that silently changed the quoting.\n\nThe test drives the behaviour through pmLaunchPlan rather than calling quoteWindowsArg directly, which keeps the helper private and exercises the path an actual launch takes. My first version of the assertion was wrong in a way worth recording: it placed the backslash run before the trailing text, where the run is followed by an ordinary character and therefore passes through unchanged rather than doubling. The doubling rule applies only to a run at the very end of the element, so the witness now ends with the run and exercises both the quadratic path and the doubling.\n\nGates after the change: 256 tests pass with 0 failures, the coverage gate reports thresholds met, and pm health --strict-exit exits 0 under this repository's own pinned 2026.8.31 binary rather than the global CLI."
  "2026-09-03T18:45:48.703Z",codex,"Both review findings this round were valid and are fixed.\n\nThe wall-clock assertion was replaced rather than loosened. Greptile's point is sharper here than in a pure Linux repository, because this suite also runs on a Windows launcher job, so a single cold 50ms measurement carries JIT warm-up, GC pauses, scheduler noise, coverage instrumentation and a slower runner all inside it. A bound that can fail on a correct implementation gets raised or deleted the first time it flakes, which is how a regression test stops guarding anything. The test now warms the JIT, then asserts an absolute 2000ms bound the linear pass cannot approach on any runner, plus the scale-free claim that doubling the backslash run must not multiply the time superlinearly. The ratio is skipped when the single measurement is under a millisecond, because dividing by noise manufactures a huge ratio on an idle machine.\n\nRe-verified against the new bound rather than assuming the old proof carried: restoring the original two replace calls fails at 12861.81ms against the 2000ms bound. The correctness assertion on the doubling rule is retained alongside the timing, so a rewrite that were fast but silently changed the quoting still fails.\n\nThe closure metadata gap was also real. The close event carried close_reason without the structured resolution, expected-result and actual-result fields that pm-cli 2026.9.1 added, and all three are now recorded through pm update rather than by editing the append-only hash-chained history."
tests[1]{command,path,scope,timeout_seconds,provenance{author,created_at,source_kind,source_ref},note}:
  "npx tsx --test --test-name-pattern \"the win32 tail is built in linear time\" test/fetch-all-items.test.ts",test/fetch-all-items.test.ts,project,180,codex,"2026-09-03T17:57:03.629Z",local_mutation,main,"ReDoS regression: proved to fail at 16202ms against the original two replace calls"
docs[1]{path,scope,note}:
